1|0@politics^1440198424#cub8c51:Kirkayak$:&gt;A newborn infant does not even yet have the capacity to do things that pigs or dogs can do, and certainly not things that adult humans can do, and yet they are treated like other humans (right to life, medical care, citizenship, etc). A newborn is *both* human and a person. (I would argue that) a 2 month old fetus is a human (per DNA), but not a person (it is not developed enough). While a pig or dog is not a human, they ought to be protected from cruelty (because highly sensitive to pain). A newborn infant can both feel pain (sensitively) and cognate (albeit in a limited fashion)... but most importantly, is not living within the visceral innards of a female. I hold that a right to suicide by a non-deranged individual is absolute, and since a non-viable fetus cannot survive outside of the womb, it stands to reason (for someone holding my view of suicide) that a pregnant female should have 100% sovereignty over the contents of her viscera (provided the technology exists to control such) until such time that a fetus is viable (irrespective of whether she commits suicide or not)... and even after that point, all that should be mandatory of a female seeking to die before coming to term is to suffer our *attempt* to rescue said fetus (NOT ensure a rescue), via C-section, before she "shuffles off the mortal coil". &gt;If "personhood" is arbitrary anyway, why not play it safe and claim that personhood is inherently a quality of every human? If I (and others) are invoking criteria such as a brain and nervous system developed enough to cognate and register pain... when attempting to discern personhood... such is certainly not arbitrary. The point is: unless one is a person who is invested in the idea of a soul (empirically undetected thus far, yet also one of those infinite number of things classified as an unfalsifiable unknown), I very much doubt that one should consider a 4 day old zygote to be a person... and yet, a 34 week old fetus will almost invariably be considered a person by most reasonable people. There MUST be switch-over criteria of some sort, within that interim.
